5, but not Examples 1 and 2, which have no complement. A \"gloss/alias\" rule explains 1 and 2, but not 3–5. The shared factor is that the span describes a pathological state instead of naming the disorder, and that state is what the disease is defined by or known as. Example 3 also contrasts with the TP \"G6PD **deficient** phenotype\", which is labeled Modifier. The adjectival \"deficient\" is a Modifier, while the nominal \"deficiency\" heads a disease.\n\n**Step 2: Pattern Insight**\n\n- **Pattern Name:** Disease Named by Its Defect or Lesion State (Descriptive Alias vs. Nominal Label)\n- **Confusion Trigger:** Nosological-state nouns used descriptively (\"deficiency/deficient activity **of/in** [enzyme]\", \"telangiectasis\" as a gloss). The model treats these as biochemical mechanisms or morphological signs and tags them O. This is reinforced when the head is not \"disease/syndrome\" and the defect is introduced by a preposition, a parenthetical, or \"so-called.\"\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** True Positives use a nominal disease label. The enzyme or gene appears as a premodifier (\"G6PD deficiency\") or the disorder is named by an eponym or acronym (\"Coats disease\", \"SLS\"). They do not rely on an \"of/in\" complement or a gloss to name the disorder.\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Tag a deficiency or lesion phrase as SpecificDisease, with the full span including its complement, when it is the *name or definition of the disorder itself*. This applies when (a) it is an alias or appositive co-referring with a disease name in the same sentence or passage (\"so-called X\", \"(X)\"), or (b) it is the defining phenotype of a named disease in a \"disease is caused by / characterized by\" frame (\"PKU caused by a deficiency of PAH\"). Do not tag it when the state is a downstream molecular mechanism of a disease, such as \"results in a deficiency of norrin,\" or when the adjectival \"deficient\" modifies a non-disease head, which is a Modifier.", "type": "text"}]